      "id": 1,
      "text": "Orleans was platted March 11 1815 by William McFarland and Samuel Lewis, becoming Orange County's oldest town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/orleans_indiana",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town was named to honor the Battle of New Orleans, commemorating Andrew Jackson's 1815 victory over the British.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/orleans_indiana",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A historic central square, originally called Congress Square, hosts a 1926 brick bandstand and vintage gas lights.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://town.orleans.in.us/history/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1851‑52 the New Albany and Salem Railroad was routed through Orleans after citizens raised $40,000, spurring growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
